{# Content grid - rendered in place of the plain block column when the post
   configures one. `grid` comes from GridViewBuilder and is null when disabled
   or empty, so the caller guards on it rather than this file second-guessing.

   Zones lay out on the shared 48-column grid (`.aurora-grid`, see
   css/base/aurora-grid.css), the same one the banner uses. Each one carries
   its width as custom properties rather than classes: a span is a runtime
   number, and Tailwind only emits classes it can read in the source.

   `startStyle` carries where the zone lands - its row and its first column,
   worked out by GridNormalizer::place from the sequence the author wrote, and
   emitted for the large screen only. `|default('')` rather than a bare read:
   a theme rendering a grid built before this existed passes zones without the
   key, and they should go on flowing rather than raise.

   Gutters come from the grid's own item padding. A `gap-x` here would be 47
   gutters across the 48 tracks - see AuroraGridGutterTest.

   Every zone that is not text carries `not-prose`. The grid renders inside
   `<article class="prose">`, and Typography styles what it finds there: a card
   image was getting `margin: 2em 0` from `.prose :where(img)`, an iframe the
   same. Not on the container, on each zone - the exclusion is written
   `[class~=not-prose] *`, so a `not-prose` wrapper would also switch off the
   `prose` that the text zones deliberately switch back on.

   `aurora-grid-flush` pulls the two outer edges back by that same gutter, so
   the first column lines up with the title above the grid rather than sitting
   a gutter to the right of it. #}

{% import 'Frontend/themes/default/editorial/post/_grid_surface.html.twig' as surface %}

{# `scroll-mt-24` only where there is an anchor: the header is fixed, so a
   browser told to jump to an element puts it under the bar unless the element
   reserves the room itself. 6rem is the bar plus the breathing space the
   summary already uses for its own sticky offset. #}
<div class="aurora-grid aurora-grid-flush gap-y-8">
    {% for zone in grid.zones %}
        {# `data-reveal` sur l'enveloppe et non à l'intérieur : elle contient
           la zone *et* le fond sur lequel elle est posée, donc la carte arrive
           avec son contenu au lieu que le texte glisse dans un cadre déjà là.

           Émis seulement quand il y a un effet. Un attribut à `none` sur
           chaque zone ferait travailler l'observateur pour rien, et
           `|default('none')` couvre les grilles écrites avant que la clé
           existe. #}
        {% set reveal = zone.reveal|default('none') %}
        <div
            {% if zone.anchor|default('') %}id="{{ zone.anchor }}" class="scroll-mt-24"{% endif %}
            {% if reveal != 'none' %}data-reveal="{{ reveal }}"{% endif %}
            style="{{ zone.spanStyle|raw }}{{ zone.startStyle|default('')|raw }}"
        >
            {% if zone.type == 'stack' %}
                {# Captured and wrapped like a leaf zone, so a stack can sit on
                   a surface too. That is what a call to action is made of - a
                   title, a line and a button in one band - and without it the
                   three would be three zones with nothing holding them
                   together. #}
                {% set stackBody %}
                {# A column of zones sharing the height of the row. It gets that
                   height for free: grid items stretch to their row, so the stack
                   is already as tall as its tallest neighbour and only has to
                   divide what it was given.

                   `h-full` is what passes that height on - without it the column
                   would be as tall as its content and the shares would divide
                   nothing. The gap matches the row gap above, so a stack reads
                   as part of the same lattice rather than as a tighter island. #}
                <div class="flex h-full flex-col gap-y-8">
                    {# `lg:min-h-0` only for a zone that fills. Everywhere else
                       the automatic floor is the whole point: a flex item does
                       not shrink under its own content, which is what keeps
                       text from being clipped when its share is smaller than
                       what it has to say. A zone that asks to fill has already
                       consented to being cropped, and without lifting the floor
                       it does the opposite of what it says - the picture
                       imposes its natural height and its neighbours give way. #}
                    {% for child in zone.children %}
                        {# Un enfant de pile a son propre effet, et c'est
                           volontaire : deux moitiés qui se rejoignent, l'une
                           par la gauche et l'autre par la droite, est
                           précisément ce qu'une pile permet d'écrire. Si la
                           pile en porte un elle-même, les deux se jouent, la
                           sienne portant l'ensemble. #}
                        {% set childReveal = child.reveal|default('none') %}
                        <div
                            {% if child.anchor|default('') %}id="{{ child.anchor }}"{% endif %}
                            {% if childReveal != 'none' %}data-reveal="{{ childReveal }}"{% endif %}
                            class="min-w-0{{ child.ratio == 'fill' ? ' lg:min-h-0' : '' }}{{ child.anchor|default('') ? ' scroll-mt-24' : '' }}"
                            style="{{ child.shareStyle|raw }}"
                        >
                            {{ include('Frontend/themes/default/editorial/post/_grid_zone.html.twig', {zone: child, locale: locale}) }}
                        </div>
                    {% endfor %}
                </div>
                {% endset %}
                {{ surface.wrap(zone, stackBody) }}
            {% elseif zone.sticky|default(false) %}
                {# **Collant à l'intérieur de la cellule, pas sur la cellule.**
                   Un item de grille s'étire déjà à la hauteur de sa ligne, donc
                   il n'a nulle part où glisser : c'est lui qui fournit la
                   course, et c'est son contenu qui la parcourt.

                   `lg:` seulement. En dessous, tout est empilé en pleine
                   largeur et une zone collante n'aurait rien à laisser passer ;
                   elle resterait accrochée au-dessus d'elle-même.

                   `top-24` est la hauteur de l'entête fixe plus sa respiration,
                   la même valeur que le `scroll-mt-24` des ancres au-dessus. #}
                <div class="lg:sticky lg:top-24">
                    {{ include('Frontend/themes/default/editorial/post/_grid_zone.html.twig', {zone: zone, locale: locale}) }}
                </div>
            {% else %}
                {{ include('Frontend/themes/default/editorial/post/_grid_zone.html.twig', {zone: zone, locale: locale}) }}
            {% endif %}
        </div>
    {% endfor %}
</div>

{# The overlay, mounted once for the whole grid and only when there is a
   picture to enlarge. Its own attribute, not the gallery's: a page can have
   both, each with its own order, and one attribute for the two would open both
   overlays on two different pictures. #}
{% if grid.lightbox|default([]) is not empty %}
    <div {{ vue_component('editorial/frontend/PostGalleryLightbox', {
        items: grid.lightbox,
        trigger: 'data-grid-image-open',
    }) }}></div>
{% endif %}
